var e=`# CHAPTER 2 — The Spoiled Girl

After Elizabeth died, my dad was left alone with a five-year-old he barely knew how to hold.

And he did what a lot of fathers do in that situation. The wrong thing.

He worked away almost all the time. Consulting — that's how he describes it, with a pause before the word ([it's the same line I use years later, main story, ch. 9](/historias/lutas-de-ilusao/capitulo-09)). We moved cities all the time, but always from one nice house to another nice house. Pool, gardener, a woman who cooked, a man who drove.

And every time he came back from a trip feeling guilty for being gone, he came back with a gift. An expensive toy. An outing. A "yes" to anything I asked for.

---

At nine, I was a little monster.

I'm not going to soften it. I was spoiled, obnoxious, and I thought I was better than everyone. I yelled at my dad's staff when the food took too long. I cried when I didn't get things right away. I treated adults like adults were a service.

I wasn't cruel. It was worse — I was certain. Certain the world worked that way, that I was at the top of it, and that anyone who wasn't serving me was doing their job wrong.

---

My dad saw all of it and did nothing.

Today I understand why. Back then I just thought that was how things worked.

Once I threw a glass on the floor on purpose because the juice wasn't the right fruit. The kitchen lady came to clean it up. My dad was standing in the doorway, watching. He didn't say anything to me. He just stood there looking at the broken glass on the floor with a face I couldn't read at the time.

I can read it now. It was fear.

He was afraid of me. Or of what I was turning into. Or that if he corrected me, I'd stop liking him.

---

I could fill a page with the things I did back then, and I'm not proud of a single one.

There was the school when I was seven, a pricey school in one of those cities, where a girl in my class brought in a new bracelet and I said in front of everyone that my mom had one just like it, only real. I didn't even have a mom. I said it anyway, because in my head, winning the conversation was worth more than anything.

There was the time I made the driver go back three blocks because I'd forgotten a toy I wasn't even going to use, and he went back, because going back was his job, and I thought that was how it should be.

There was the birthday party where I cried in the middle because the cake wasn't the flavor I'd decided on the week before and nobody had guessed I'd changed my mind.

I was unbearable. And the worst part is that nobody around me could tell me that, because everybody around me worked for me.

---

The only one who didn't work for me was my dad. And he was almost always away.

When he got home, I was sweet to him — I really did love him — but I'd already learned that he came back guilty, and guilt turns into gifts. So I'd put on my missed-you face and collect the box.

He'd watch me open the presents with a smile that never reached his eyes. I didn't notice it back then. I notice it now, from memory.

It was around that time that he decided he was going to fix it. And the way he chose was radical.
`;export{e as default};
